# Old.Barvabygden Agent Conventions

## Core priorities
- Prefer Bootstrap utilities/components before writing custom CSS or JS.
- Keep a playful, friendly visual tone where appropriate, but preserve readability and accessibility.
- Use vanilla HTML/CSS/JS patterns; do not introduce frameworks or build tools unless explicitly requested.

## Project structure and refactor direction
- Place all new non-root assets under `assets/` (pages, images, scripts, styles, data).
- Do not create new loose top-level files unless explicitly requested.
- When editing older root files, prefer incremental refactors: improve touched sections without large rewrites.
- Reuse existing `assets/` folder patterns and naming conventions before creating new ones.

## HTML conventions
- Use semantic structure first (`header`, `main`, `section`, `article`, `footer`).
- Prefer Bootstrap grid/layout and spacing utilities over custom layout classes.
- Keep markup clean and component-like; avoid deep nesting when possible.
- Use descriptive `alt` text and meaningful labels for interactive elements.

## CSS conventions
- Only add custom CSS when Bootstrap cannot solve the need cleanly.
- Keep custom selectors scoped and specific to the page/feature.
- Avoid `!important` unless overriding legacy code is unavoidable.
- Keep visual style consistent with existing typography, spacing, and color choices.

## JavaScript conventions
- Keep scripts modular and simple: small functions, clear naming, minimal global scope.
- Use event delegation for repeated interactive elements when practical.
- Do not add heavy dependencies for behavior solvable in vanilla JS.
- Fail gracefully: guard DOM queries and avoid runtime errors on missing elements.

## Editing behavior for agents
- Preserve existing behavior unless the task explicitly asks for changes.
- Keep diffs focused: modify only files relevant to the request.
- For legacy sections, improve clarity and consistency as you touch them; avoid broad unrelated refactors.
- If a structural convention is unclear, follow existing local patterns in the nearest related file.
